{"seconds": 248.5, "exit": 0, "final": "Good! After **3. Kxg2**, White is **+5** material (the rook captured minus the bishop and pawns traded). White has won a full rook for a bishop and pawn\u2014that's about +2 material advantage, which is clearly winning.\n\nSo the main line is:\n- **1. Bxe5** \n  - If **1...Qc5+ 2. bxc5**, White wins the queen (+9)\n  - If **1...Rxg2+ 2. Qxg2 Rxg2+ 3. Kxg2**, White is up about +2 material and has the better position\n\nThis is the clearly best move. The key motif is that the bishop on e5 is a **hanging piece** that Black's queen is defending. When captured, Black is forced into tactics that all lose material. When Black tries to create counterplay with Qc5+ or Rxg2+, they either lose the queen or end up down material anyway.\n\n**Best move: Bxe5**\n\n**Main line: 1. Bxe5 Qc5+ 2. bxc5** (winning Black's queen)\n\n**Motif: hangingPiece** (the bishop on e5 is undefended despite being attacked only by the queen, and when taken, Black's defensive tries all fail)\n\n**Coaching note for 1500-rated player:**\n\nWhen your opponent's piece (like the bishop on e5) is only defended by one piece, capture it! Even if the defender can give checks, the forced sequence usually wins material. Here, after Bxe5, Black's queen must move\u2014if it checks on c5, the pawn on b4 catches it. Spot undefended pieces defended by only the queen: that's where the tactics live.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Bxe5\", \"line\": [\"Bxe5\", \"Qc5+\", \"bxc5\"], \"motifs\": [\"hangingPiece\", \"deflection\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r3k1r1/1p2qp1p/2p5/4b3/pP3p2/P2B3P/1B1Q2P1/5RK1 w q - 0 26"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r3k1r1/1p2qp1p/2p5/4b3/pP3p2/P2B3P/1B1Q2P1/5RK1 w q - 0 26"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "r3k1r1/1p2qp1p/2p5/4b3/pP3p2/P2B3P/1B1Q2P1/5RK1 w q - 0 26"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r3k1r